BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Intro to Wheel Throwing with Naomi & Jack — Sebastopol Center Fo
 r the Arts\, California
DTSTART:20260521T000000
DTEND:20260522T000000
UID:c3beddb404864f1b97bd80bf83590ab9@sebarts.org
DESCRIPTION:Intro to Wheel Throwing with Naomi & Jack\n\n  \n    \n      \
 n        \n          $295.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nThursdays\, Ma
 y 21 -June 25\, 2026\; 2-5pm (6 weeks)$250 + $45 Clay Fee (max 8 students)
 This course is designed for students who are new to the potter’s wheel a
 nd eager to discover the fundamentals of wheel throwing. With an emphasis 
 on core technique\, each session builds a solid foundation in the essentia
 l skills of centering\, opening\, pulling up walls\, trimming and glazing 
 — the building blocks that every potter returns to again and again.Each 
 class begins with a demonstration focused on one or more of these foundati
 onal techniques\, giving students a clear picture of the process before ge
 tting their hands in the clay. From there\, students work at their own whe
 els with plenty of time to practice\, explore and develop their feel for t
 he material. The goal is for each person to leave the course being able to
  make a thrown piece — a bowl\, a mug\, a vase — that is genuinely the
 ir own\, shaped by their emerging skills and their personal sense of what 
 they love about pottery.With two teachers in the room\, there is ample opp
 ortunity for individual attention and hands-on guidance at every stage of 
 the process. At the same time\, there is real joy in learning alongside ot
 hers — watching a classmate pull up a wall for the first time\, or seein
 g a finished glaze come out of the kiln — and that shared experience is 
 very much a part of what makes this class special.No prior experience is n
 ecessary. All you need is curiosity and a willingness to get a little mudd
 y.About the Instructors: Naomi Fuchs brings a deep love of clay and a genu
 ine passion for sharing the experience with others to everything she does 
 in the clay room. She believes that each pot is an opportunity to learn 
 — about the nature of clay\, and about yourself. Naomi has been throwing
  on the wheel for four years\, and in that time the practice has become a 
 grounding\, peaceful and creative part of her life that has deepened her c
 onnection to art and making. Her warm\, encouraging approach creates a spa
 ce where beginners feel supported as they find their footing at the wheel 
 and begin to discover what clay can do in their hands.In just one year\, p
 ottery has become one of Jack Harrison’s passions—a craft that continu
 es to humble him\, calm him\, and inspire him as he shapes both the clay a
 nd his own journey. Throwing vases is what he does most\, but he is experi
 enced in all areas of the wheel. Sharing his passion with others in the st
 udio has allowed him to become part of a community that supports and chall
 enges each other. As a mellow\, focused individual\, beginners will find c
 omfort in learning from him.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n
 \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n          \n            \n              \n            
     Add to cart
